 \chapter{Digital Control and Firmware}
 \label{ch:control}
+
+Firmware is the program run by the microcontroller (MCU). It reads the sensing inputs, decides how much switching is needed, and commands the driver through output pins. The labels PA0, PA1, and PA2 identify the sensing connections for primary voltage, secondary-bulk voltage, and primary half-bridge current. On the G071, PA8 and PA9 carry the switching commands and PA10 enables their bursts. These names identify MCU connections; they are not measured voltages or currents by themselves. The basic pin naming and signal paths are introduced in \cref{sec:intro-controller-basics}.
 
 The hardware could transfer power, but a detachable load required more than a fixed switching command. At startup, the secondary supply must first be charged before valid feedback is available. Transfer must then be increased under load and reduced after load removal. The carrier was kept fixed, and enable bursts were used to meet these requirements. This chapter covers the PWR-1.9.20 controller used for the final measurements, followed by the PWR-2.0.1 port for V2 hardware and browser logging. Both complete sources are included in Appendix~\ref{app:full-embedded-source}.
 
